\begin{abstract}
We study the relationship between path-connectivity in Vietoris--Rips graphs and the underlying metric distance, and identify precisely the geometric hypothesis under which the two coincide. In an arbitrary pseudometric space, connectivity of two points $x,y$ in the Rips graph at scale $\eps$ controls the true distance only through the weak \emph{Archimedean} bound $\dist(x,y) \le n\eps$, where $n$ is the number of edges traversed; the factor $n$ encodes the ``Archimedean leak'' whereby chains of short edges accumulate without bound. Our main theorem is that this leak is sealed completely under the \emph{strong (ultrametric) triangle inequality} $\dist(x,z) \le \max(\dist(x,y),\dist(y,z))$. Over any ultrametric (non-Archimedean / valuation) space, Rips reachability at scale $\eps \ge 0$ is \emph{equivalent} to the single sublevel test $\dist(x,y) \le \eps$; the entire path collapses to one edge. Consequently the connectivity classes are exactly closed metric balls, and the connectivity threshold $\connThreshold(x,y) := \dist(x,y)$ itself satisfies the strong triangle inequality, i.e.\ it is a structure-preserving map into the tropical (max-plus) semiring. We frame this as a \emph{functorial tropical lower bound}: $\dist(x,y)$ is a certified, tight lower bound on every scale capable of connecting $x$ and $y$. We give complete proof sketches, an algorithmic treatment via single-linkage / minimum spanning trees, numerical demonstrations, and applications to topological data analysis, hierarchical clustering, and $p$-adic geometry. All results have been formally verified.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} Vietoris--Rips complex, ultrametric, non-Archimedean geometry, persistent homology, tropical semiring, max-plus algebra, single-linkage clustering, bottleneck distance, $p$-adic valuation.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Motivation}
The Vietoris--Rips construction is among the most widely used tools in topological data analysis (TDA). Given a finite metric sample $X$ and a scale parameter $\eps$, one forms a graph (its $1$-skeleton) by joining points within distance $\eps$, and studies how the topology of the resulting filtration evolves as $\eps$ increases. The degree-zero (connected-components) information of this filtration is exactly the \emph{single-linkage hierarchical clustering} of $X$: as $\eps$ grows, clusters merge, and the record of merge-scales is a dendrogram.

A subtle but fundamental phenomenon governs this process. Path-connectivity in the Rips graph is a \emph{transitive closure}: two points are connected if there is \textbf{any} chain of short edges between them, however long. The length of that chain can be enormous, and the endpoints can be far apart even though every edge is short. We call this the \textbf{Archimedean leak}: in an Archimedean metric, $n$ steps of size $\eps$ can span a distance up to $n\eps$. This is the \emph{chaining effect} that makes single-linkage clustering notoriously sensitive to bridges of intermediate points.

The purpose of this paper is to pin down exactly when the leak disappears---when connectivity becomes a faithful, leak-free measurement of distance---and to recognize the resulting structure as a clean instance of tropical (max-plus) algebra.

\subsection{Summary of results}
Let $(\alpha, \dist)$ be a pseudometric space and let $\Rips_\eps(\alpha)$ denote the Rips graph at scale $\eps$, with adjacency $x \sim y \iff x \ne y \wedge \dist(x,y) \le \eps$. Write $\Reach_\eps(x,y)$ for path-connectivity (reachability).
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Filtration monotonicity} (Thm.~\ref{thm:mono}): $\eps_1 \le \eps_2 \Rightarrow \Rips_{\eps_1}(\alpha) \subseteq \Rips_{\eps_2}(\alpha)$.
  \item \textbf{Functoriality of connectivity} (Thm.~\ref{thm:reachmono}): reachability is monotone in $\eps$.
  \item \textbf{The Archimedean bound} (Thm.~\ref{thm:arch}): a walk of length $n$ certifies only $\dist(x,y) \le n\eps$.
  \item \textbf{Ultrametric collapse} (Thm.~\ref{thm:collapse}): if $\alpha$ is ultrametric and $\eps \ge 0$, $\Reach_\eps(x,y) \Rightarrow \dist(x,y) \le \eps$.
  \item \textbf{Connectivity $=$ sublevel test} (Thm.~\ref{thm:iff}): over an ultrametric space, $\Reach_\eps(x,y) \iff \dist(x,y) \le \eps$.
  \item \textbf{Connectivity classes are balls} (Thm.~\ref{thm:ball}): the reachable set of $x$ equals $\cball(x,\eps)$.
  \item \textbf{The tropical threshold functor} (Thms.~\ref{thm:trop}, \ref{thm:lb}): $\connThreshold = \dist$ satisfies the tropical triangle inequality and is the tight lower bound on connecting scale.
\end{enumerate}
The crucial dichotomy is between item~3 ($\dist \le n\eps$) and items~4--5 ($\dist \le \eps$): ultrametricity deletes the multiplier $n$.

\subsection{The bridge}
The paper sits at the intersection of three strands: \emph{metric filtrations / Vietoris--Rips graphs} (the topological side), \emph{tropical max-plus valuation algebra} (the algebraic side, where the arithmetic of bottlenecks lives), and \emph{ultrametric / valuation depth} (the number-theoretic side: $p$-adic valuations, non-Archimedean fields). The ultrametric collapse is the hinge connecting all three.

\section{Preliminaries and Definitions}

\begin{definition}[Pseudometric space]
A \emph{pseudometric space} is a set $\alpha$ with $\dist : \alpha \times \alpha \to \R_{\ge 0}$ satisfying $\dist(x,x)=0$, symmetry, and the triangle inequality $\dist(x,z) \le \dist(x,y)+\dist(y,z)$. Distinct points may be at distance $0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Ultrametric / non-Archimedean space]
A pseudometric space $\alpha$ is \emph{ultrametric} if it satisfies the \textbf{strong triangle inequality}
\[ \dist(x,z) \le \max\bigl(\dist(x,y),\,\dist(y,z)\bigr) \qquad \forall x,y,z. \]
Since $\max(a,b)\le a+b$ for $a,b\ge 0$, every ultrametric space is pseudometric; the converse fails.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}
(i) Any discrete metric. (ii) $\Qp$ with $\dist(x,y)=p^{-v_p(x-y)}$, $v_p$ the $p$-adic valuation. (iii) The leaves of a rooted weighted tree under lowest-common-ancestor height (any dendrogram). (iv) Strings under the longest-common-prefix metric. In an ultrametric space every triangle is isosceles with shortest base, and closed balls are nested (disjoint or comparable)---the origin of tree-like structure.
\end{example}

\begin{definition}[Rips graph]
For a pseudometric space $\alpha$ and scale $\eps \in \R$, the \emph{Rips graph} $\Rips_\eps(\alpha)$ is the simple graph on $\alpha$ with adjacency
\[ x \sim_\eps y \quad :\Longleftrightarrow\quad x \ne y \ \wedge\ \dist(x,y) \le \eps. \]
It is symmetric and loopless, hence a well-defined simple graph.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Walk, reachability]
A \emph{walk} of length $n$ from $x$ to $y$ is a sequence $x=v_0 \sim_\eps v_1 \sim_\eps \cdots \sim_\eps v_n=y$. Points are \emph{reachable}, $\Reach_\eps(x,y)$, if some walk (possibly empty when $x=y$) connects them. Reachability is the equivalence relation generated by adjacency; its classes are the connected components.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Connectivity threshold]
The \emph{connectivity threshold} is $\connThreshold(x,y) := \dist(x,y)$. The name is justified by the main theorem: over an ultrametric space this is the infimal scale at which $x,y$ become reachable.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Max-plus semiring]
The \emph{tropical max-plus semiring} is $(\R\cup\{-\infty\},\oplus,\odot)$ with $a\oplus b := \max(a,b)$ and $a\odot b := a+b$. Inequalities $f(x,z)\le \max(f(x,y),f(y,z))$ are precisely the tropical triangle inequality: $f$ lands compatibly in the $\oplus=\max$ structure.
\end{definition}

\section{Functoriality of the Filtration}

\begin{theorem}[Filtration monotonicity]\label{thm:mono}
If $\eps_1 \le \eps_2$ then $\Rips_{\eps_1}(\alpha) \le \Rips_{\eps_2}(\alpha)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $x\sim_{\eps_1}y$ then $\dist(x,y)\le\eps_1\le\eps_2$, so $x\sim_{\eps_2}y$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Monotonicity of reachability]\label{thm:reachmono}
If $\eps_1\le\eps_2$ and $\Reach_{\eps_1}(x,y)$ then $\Reach_{\eps_2}(x,y)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A walk in $\Rips_{\eps_1}$ is edge-by-edge a walk in $\Rips_{\eps_2}$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:mono}.
\end{proof}

\noindent These express that $\eps \mapsto \Rips_\eps(\alpha)$ is a functor from $(\R,\le)$ to graphs and onward to $\pi_0$---the standard filtration functoriality underlying persistent homology.

\section{The General (Archimedean) Bound}

\begin{theorem}[Archimedean leak bound]\label{thm:arch}
In an arbitrary pseudometric space, if there is a walk of length $n$ from $x$ to $y$ in $\Rips_\eps(\alpha)$, then $\dist(x,y) \le n\eps$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induct on the walk. Empty walk: $\dist(x,x)=0\le 0$. For a first edge $x\sim_\eps u$ followed by a walk of length $n-1$ from $u$ to $y$, the inductive hypothesis gives $\dist(u,y)\le(n-1)\eps$ and the edge gives $\dist(x,u)\le\eps$; the ordinary triangle inequality yields $\dist(x,y)\le \eps+(n-1)\eps = n\eps$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Tightness]
On $\R$, the points $0,\eps,2\eps,\dots,n\eps$ form a walk at scale $\eps$ with endpoints exactly $n\eps$ apart. The factor $n$ is unavoidable: each step \emph{adds} $\eps$. Note $\eps\ge 0$ is not needed here.
\end{remark}

\section{The Ultrametric Collapse}

We now assume $\alpha$ is ultrametric.

\begin{theorem}[Ultrametric collapse, lower bound]\label{thm:collapse}
Let $\alpha$ be ultrametric and $\eps\ge 0$. If $\Reach_\eps(x,y)$ then $\dist(x,y)\le\eps$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Choose a witnessing walk and induct. Base case ($x=y$): $\dist(x,x)=0\le\eps$ using $\eps\ge 0$. Inductive step: a first edge $x\sim_\eps u$ gives $\dist(x,u)\le\eps$, the inductive hypothesis gives $\dist(u,y)\le\eps$, and the \emph{strong} triangle inequality gives
\[ \dist(x,y) \le \max\bigl(\dist(x,u),\dist(u,y)\bigr) \le \max(\eps,\eps)=\eps. \]
The decisive difference from Theorem~\ref{thm:arch} is $\max(\eps,\eps)=\eps$ versus $\eps+\eps=2\eps$: the max does not accumulate, so arbitrarily long chains stay within $\eps$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Connectivity $=$ sublevel test]\label{thm:iff}
Let $\alpha$ be ultrametric and $\eps\ge 0$. Then $\Reach_\eps(x,y) \iff \dist(x,y)\le\eps$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Rightarrow$) is Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse}. ($\Leftarrow$): if $x=y$ the empty walk suffices; otherwise $x\sim_\eps y$ is a single edge.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Connectivity classes are closed balls]\label{thm:ball}
Let $\alpha$ be ultrametric and $\eps\ge 0$. The component of $x$ is $\{y : \Reach_\eps(x,y)\} = \cball(x,\eps) = \{y : \dist(x,y)\le\eps\}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Immediate from Theorem~\ref{thm:iff} and symmetry of $\dist$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Laminar hierarchical clustering]
Closed balls are nested, so the components over all $\eps\ge 0$ form a laminar family---a dendrogram. The Rips filtration of an ultrametric space \emph{is} its canonical hierarchical clustering.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}[Both hypotheses are load-bearing]
Without $\eps\ge 0$: the empty self-walk forces $\dist(x,x)=0\le\eps$, false for $\eps<0$. Without ultrametricity: on $\R$, $0$ connects to $n\eps$ through $0,\eps,\dots,n\eps$ yet $\dist(0,n\eps)=n\eps\gg\eps$; Theorem~\ref{thm:arch} is then best possible.
\end{remark}

\section{The Tropical Connectivity-Threshold Functor}

\begin{theorem}[Tropical triangle inequality for the threshold]\label{thm:trop}
In an ultrametric space, $\connThreshold(x,z) \le \max\bigl(\connThreshold(x,y),\connThreshold(y,z)\bigr)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By definition $\connThreshold = \dist$; this is verbatim the strong triangle inequality.
\end{proof}

\noindent The quantity \emph{produced} by the connectivity analysis obeys the very inequality that \emph{made the analysis work}: $\connThreshold$ is a tropical functor from the data cloud to max-plus algebra.

\begin{theorem}[Functorial tropical lower bound]\label{thm:lb}
Let $\alpha$ be ultrametric and $\eps\ge 0$. Then $\Reach_\eps(x,y) \iff \connThreshold(x,y)\le\eps$. Hence $\connThreshold(x,y)$ is the least scale connecting $x,y$: connecting for every $\eps\ge\connThreshold(x,y)$, and every connecting scale satisfies $\eps\ge\connThreshold(x,y)$. It is a tight, certified lower bound.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Restate Theorem~\ref{thm:iff} with $\connThreshold=\dist$; the two directions of the iff give the extremal claims.
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithms}

\subsection{Bottleneck (min--max path) distance}
For a general metric, the scale at which $x,y$ first become connected is the bottleneck distance $b(x,y) := \min_{\pi: x\to y} \max_{e\in\pi} \dist(e)$, a min--max (tropical) shortest path. The filtration connects $x,y$ at scale $\eps$ iff $b(x,y)\le\eps$.

\begin{theorem}[Bottleneck $=$ distance over ultrametrics]\label{thm:bottleneck}
If $\alpha$ is ultrametric, $b(x,y)=\dist(x,y)$ for all $x,y$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$b\le\dist$ via the one-edge path; $b\ge\dist$ since any path realizing max-edge $\eps$ exhibits reachability, so $\dist(x,y)\le\eps$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse}; minimize over paths.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Minimum spanning tree / single-linkage}
The bottleneck distances are read off from a minimum spanning tree: $b(x,y)$ is the maximum edge weight on the unique MST path (minimax property). Recording merge-scales yields the single-linkage dendrogram; over an ultrametric, Theorem~\ref{thm:bottleneck} says these merge-scales equal the pairwise distances---the dendrogram reconstructs the ultrametric exactly (the classical subdominant-ultrametric equivalence, certified exact on ultrametric inputs).

\subsection{Sublevel-graph component counting}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:iff}, $\pi_0$ of $\Rips_\eps(\alpha)$ over an ultrametric reduces to grouping by the (genuinely transitive) relation $\dist\le\eps$: a union--find pass over edges of weight $\le\eps$. The component count is antitone in $\eps$ and equals the number of distinct closed $\eps$-balls.

\section{Worked Numerical Examples}

\paragraph{$p$-adic integers.} On $\Z$ with $\dist(a,b)=2^{-v_2(a-b)}$: $\dist(0,1)=1$, $\dist(0,2)=0.5$, $\dist(0,4)=0.25$, $\dist(2,4)=0.5$. Strong inequality on $(0,2,4)$: $0.25\le\max(0.5,0.5)$. At $\eps=0.5$ the chain $0\sim 2\sim 4$ predicts $\dist(0,4)\le 0.5$, confirmed (it is $0.25$). No leak.

\paragraph{Dendrogram ultrametric.} Leaves $a,b,c,d$ with $d(a,b)=1$, $d(c,d)=2$, all cross-distances $3$. At $\eps=2$: components $\{a,b\},\{c,d\}$ (two closed $2$-balls); at $\eps=3$: one component. Recovered merge-scales: $1,2,3$.

\paragraph{Archimedean contrast.} On $\R$, points $0,1,2,3,4$ at $\eps=1$ are connected, yet $\dist(0,4)=4=4\eps$: Theorem~\ref{thm:arch} is tight and Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse} (rightly) fails, as $\R$ is not ultrametric.

\section{Applications}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{TDA:} on hierarchical data, the $\pi_0$ persistence barcode records true distances as death-scales without chaining artifacts.
  \item \textbf{Hierarchical clustering:} single-linkage computes the subdominant ultrametric; on ultrametric inputs the dendrogram reconstructs the metric exactly.
  \item \textbf{Phylogenetics:} molecular-clock distances are ultrametric; clustering recovers divergence times exactly.
  \item \textbf{$p$-adic geometry:} non-Archimedean fields are immune to chaining; connectivity equals proximity.
  \item \textbf{Tropical optimization:} the threshold is a bottleneck quantity, connecting clustering to max-plus shortest-path algebra.
\end{itemize}

\section{Discussion}
The technical heart is one substitution: bounding the distance across a walk, the ordinary triangle inequality contributes $\dist(x,u)+\dist(u,y)\le 2\eps$ while the strong inequality contributes $\max(\dist(x,u),\dist(u,y))\le\eps$. Iterated over length $n$: $n\eps$ versus $\eps$. That the ``$+$ / $\max$'' dichotomy is exactly the Archimedean/non-Archimedean and classical/tropical dichotomy makes the result a genuine bridge. We work with pseudometrics (costless generality) and keep $\eps\ge 0$ (needed only for the reflexive empty walk, but then essential). The result is sharp (Remark~6.5/counterexamples), motivating the converse conjecture.

\section{Future Directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Converse characterization]
For a pseudometric space, $\Reach_\eps(x,y)\iff\dist(x,y)\le\eps$ for all $\eps\ge 0$ and all $x,y$ is \emph{equivalent} to ultrametricity. The converse derives the strong inequality from the iff on the two-edge walk $x\to y\to z$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Functorial component-count bound]
For a finite ultrametric space, the number of components of $\Rips_\eps$ equals the number of distinct closed $\eps$-balls, is antitone in $\eps$, and is a certified lower bound for the component count of any dominating $d'\ge d$ at the same scale.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Valuation-depth $=$ persistence-length]
The valuation depth of $(x,y)$ (length of the maximal chain of nested $\eps$-balls separating them as $\eps$ ranges over $[0,\dist(x,y)]$) equals the number of distinct finite death-scales in the $\pi_0$ barcode of the Rips filtration on $\{x,y\}$ and ancestors.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Monoidality on products]
On $\alpha\times\beta$ with $\dist((a,b),(a',b'))=\max(\dist(a,a'),\dist(b,b'))$, $\connThreshold((a,b),(a',b'))=\max(\connThreshold(a,a'),\connThreshold(b,b'))$: the tropical functor preserves products.
\end{conjecture}

\section{Conclusion}
In a general metric space, Rips connectivity is a leaky proxy for distance ($\dist\le n\eps$). The strong triangle inequality of ultrametric geometry seals the leak: reachability at scale $\eps\ge 0$ is equivalent to $\dist\le\eps$, connectivity classes are exactly closed balls, and the merge-scale functor lands in the tropical max-plus semiring as a tight, certified lower bound. The ``$+$ / $\max$'' substitution is simultaneously the Archimedean/non-Archimedean and classical/tropical dichotomy, making the collapse a faithful bridge between metric filtrations, valuation geometry, and tropical algebra. All results have been formally verified.

